 # Tree-based structures (Octree, Pointer Tree, Sparse Voxel DAG)
 
-Also called: Sparse Voxel Trees, Hierarchical Voxel Structures, Voxel Hierarchies
+Also called: Hierarchical Sparse Voxel Structures, Tree-based Sparse Volumes
 
 ## Overview
-Tree-based structures represent volumetric data using recursive spatial subdivision, where empty or uniform regions are collapsed into single nodes, providing compression proportional to data sparsity [S2, 2792-octree-based-structures.md]. This family solves the memory and bandwidth bottlenecks of dense voxel grids by enabling efficient culling of empty space and adaptive resolution [2792-octree-based-structures.md]. Unlike sibling structures like Hash Grids or Brick Maps, standard tree-based methods rely on explicit hierarchical traversal (pointers or indices) rather than hash lookups or fixed-size blocks, prioritizing spatial locality and topological flexibility over constant-time access or out-of-core simplicity [2792-octree-based-structures.md, 4983-sparse-voxel-tree-svo-tree.md].
+Tree-based structures represent volumetric data using a recursive spatial subdivision where empty or uniform regions are collapsed into single nodes, providing compression proportional to data sparsity [2792-octree-based-structures.md]. This family solves the memory and bandwidth bottlenecks of dense voxel grids by enabling efficient culling of empty space and adaptive resolution. Unlike sibling structures like [Hash Grids](936-hash-grids.md) or [SparseGrid](904-sparsegrid.md), which rely on flat hashing or fixed brick maps, tree-based structures prioritize hierarchical traversal and spatial locality, allowing for variable branching factors and explicit parent-child relationships [2792-octree-based-structures.md].
 
 ## Shared principles
 All structures in this family share the following mechanism:
 
-1.  **Recursive Subdivision:** The 3D domain is recursively divided into sub-volumes. In standard octrees, each node has 8 children. In pointer trees, the branching factor $N$ varies ($N^3$ children) [2792-octree-based-structures.md].
-2.  **Sparsity Encoding:** Empty regions are represented by null pointers or absence of children, rather than storing zero-valued voxels. This provides compression proportional to the sparsity of the data [2792-octree-based-structures.md].
-3.  **Hierarchical Traversal:** Rendering or access requires traversing from the root to the leaf. This allows skipping large empty volumes (culling) and adapting resolution to view distance or detail requirements [2792-octree-based-structures.md].
